"towards them"],["לְחַזֵּק","lechazek","H2388","HR/Vpcc","to make strong"],["יְדֵיהֶם","yedeihem","H3027","HNcbdc/Sp3mp","hands their"],["בִּמְלֶאכֶת","bimlekhet","H4399","HR/Ncfsc","in [the] work of"],["בֵּית","beit-","H1004","HNcmsc","[the] house of"],["הָאֱלֹהִים","ha'elohim","H430","HTd/Ncmpa","<the> God"],["אֱלֹהֵי","'elohei","H430","HNcmpc","[the] God of"],["יִשְׂרָאֵל","yisra'el","H3478","HNpl","Israel"]],"x":[[14030021,14030021,9],[15001001,15001001,9],[20021001,20021001,9],[14035017,14035017,8],[15007027,15007027,8],[20016007,20016007,8],[2012015,2012020,7],[2013006,2013007,7],[43019011,43019011,7],[12023029,12023029,6]],"xn":15}},"people":[{"id":"darius_978","name":"Darius","also":"","summary":"King of Persia.","verses":16,"father":"","mother":"","v":[1,12,13,14,15]},{"id":"cyrus_968","name":"Cyrus","also":"","summary":"(Heb. Ko’resh), the celebrated “King of Persia” (Elam) who was conqueror of Babylon, and issued the decree of liberation to the Jews (Ezra 1:1, 2).","verses":19,"father":"","mother":"","v":[3,14]},{"id":"nebuchadnezzar_2167","name":"Nebuchadnezzar","also":"Nebuchadrezzar","summary":"In the Babylonian orthography Nabu-kudur-uzur, which means “Nebo, protect the crown!” or the “frontiers.” In an inscription he styles himself “Nebo’s favourite.”","verses":88,"father":"","mother":"","v":[5]},{"id":"shethar-boznai_2674","name":"Shethar-boznai","also":"","summary":"Star of splendour, a Persian officer who vainly attempted to hinder the rebuilding of the temple (Ezra 5:3, 6; 6:6, 13).","verses":4,"father":"","mother":"","v":[6,13]},{"id":"tatnai_2831","name":"Tatnai","also":"","summary":"Gift, a Persian governor (Heb. pehah, i.e., “satrap;” modern “pasha”) “on this side the river”, i.e., of the whole tract on the west of the Euphrates.","verses":4,"father":"","mother":"","v":[6,13]},{"id":"haggai_1349","name":"Haggai","also":"","summary":"Festive, one of the twelve so-called minor prophets. He was the first of the three (Zechariah, his contemporary, and Malachi, who was about one hundred years later, being the other two) whose ministry belonged to the period of Jewish history which began after the return from captivity in Babylon.","verses":11,"father":"","mother":"","v":[14]},{"id":"artaxerxes_314","name":"Artaxerxes","also":"","summary":"The king mentioned in Ezra 7:1, in the seventh year (B.C. 458) of whose reign Ezra led a second colony of Jews back to Jerusalem, was probably Longimanus, who reigned for forty years (B.C. 464-425); the grandson of Darius, who, fourteen years later, permitted Nehemiah to return and rebuild Jerusalem.","verses":10,"father":"","mother":"","v":[14]},{"id":"zechariah_3016","name":"Zechariah","also":"","summary":"A prophet of Judah, the eleventh of the twelve minor prophets. Like Ezekiel, he was of priestly extraction. He describes himself (1:1) as “the son of Berechiah.”","verses":6,"father":"Berechiah","mother":"","v":[14]},{"id":"iddo_1595","name":"Iddo","also":"","summary":"","verses":4,"father":"","mother":"","v":[14]},{"id":"israel_682","name":"Israel","also":"","summary":"One who follows on another’s heels; supplanter, (Gen. 25:26; 27:36; Hos. 12:2-4), the second born of the twin sons of Isaac by Rebekah. He was born probably at Lahai-roi, when his father was fifty-nine and Abraham one hundred and fifty-nine years old.","verses":1009,"father":"Isaac","mother":"Rebekah","v":[16,21]},{"id":"moses_2108","name":"Moses","also":"","summary":"Drawn (or Egypt. mesu, “son;” hence Rameses, royal son). On the invitation of Pharaoh (Gen. 45:17-25), Jacob and his sons went down into Egypt.","verses":774,"father":"Amram","mother":"Jochebed","v":[18]}],"places":[{"id":"babylonia_152","name":"Babylonia","kind":"Region","lat":32.5,"lon":44.5,"summary":"","verses":6,"v":[1]},{"id":"ecbatana_353","name":"Ecbatana","kind":"Settlement","lat":34.79831,"lon":48.51497,"summary":"(Ezra 6:2), called Ecbatana by classical writers, the capital of northern Media. Here was the palace which was the residence of the old Median monarchs, and of Cyrus and Cambyses.","verses":1,"v":[2]},{"id":"media_776","name":"Media","kind":"Region (Country)","lat":34.5,"lon":46.5,"summary":"Heb. Madai, which is rendered in the Authorized Version (1) “Madai,” Gen. 10:2; (2) “Medes,” 2 Kings 17:6; 18:11; (3) “Media,” Esther 1:3; 10:2; Isa. 21:2; Dan. 8:20; (4) “Mede,” only in Dan. 11:1.","verses":8,"v":[2]},{"id":"jerusalem_636","name":"Jerusalem","kind":"City","lat":31.77667,"lon":35.23417,"summary":"Called also Salem, Ariel, Jebus, the “city of God,” the “holy city;” by the modern Arabs el-Khuds, meaning “the holy;” once “the city of Judah” (2 Chr. 25:28).","verses":754,"v":[3,5,9,12,18]},{"id":"babylon_151","name":"Babylon","kind":"City","lat":32.53617,"lon":44.42082,"summary":"The Greek form of BABEL; Semitic form Babilu, meaning “The Gate of God.” In the Assyrian tablets it means “The city of the dispersion of the tribes.”","verses":255,"v":[5]},{"id":"river_244","name":"river","kind":"Region","lat":31.77744,"lon":35.23494,"summary":"region","verses":16,"v":[6,8,13]},{"id":"persia_938","name":"Persia","kind":"Region (Country)","lat":34.79831,"lon":48.51497,"summary":"An ancient empire, extending from the Indus to Thrace, and from the Caspian Sea to the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf. The Persians were originally a Medic tribe which settled in Persia, on the eastern side of the Persian Gulf.","verses":25,"v":[14]},{"id":"assyria_111","name":"Assyria","kind":"Region","lat":36.50969,"lon":43.2282,"summary":"The name derived from the city Asshur on the Tigris, the original capital of the country, was originally a colony from Babylonia, and was ruled by viceroys from that kingdom.","verses":116,"v":[22]}],"events":[],"traditions":[],"sources":["JFB","MHCC","Wesley"],"tags":{"bsb":[[116,4430,1868,7761,1240,1240,1005,1005,1005,5182,1596,1596,1596,895,895],[4040,4040,4040,7912,7912,1001,1001,1001,307,307,4083,4083,4083,4076,4076,3652,3652,3652,3790,1459,1459,1799],[2298,2298,2298,8140,44